## Literature boundary

Primary literature supports the experiment geometry only:

- binary odor mixtures can show concentration-dependent synergy or masking;
- component identification changes with adaptation, intensity, and time;
- specific mixtures can be configural or elemental, and training can alter component recognition;
- component ratio can alter configural perception in an animal model.

These findings justify controlled ratios, component-alone controls, timepoints, blinding, and explicit null/collision outcomes. They do **not** predict a Suederal/Castoreum result, establish beauty, establish a safe dose, validate broad appeal, or authorize physical execution.

Primary sources checked:

- Ishii et al. 2008, DOI `10.1093/chemse/bjn022`
- Frank et al. 2010, DOI `10.1093/chemse/bjq078`
- Barkat et al. 2012, DOI `10.1093/chemse/bjr086`
- Coureaud et al. 2011, DOI `10.1093/chemse/bjr049`

